# Fires only once runner-backup.service has genuinely exhausted its bounded retries (OnFailure= is
# not invoked for a run systemd is about to auto-restart, only for one it has truly given up on). A
# template unit (the trailing "@") so OnFailure=...@%n.service can pass the failed unit's name
# through as this unit's %i.
#
# Deliberately minimal: journald alone is not an alert, but this project has no existing
# paging/notification infrastructure. Logs at `crit` priority, distinct from ordinary backup
# output, so `journalctl -p crit` (or any syslog-forwarding already configured) surfaces it
# distinctly. Recommended, not yet wired up: add a second `ExecStart=` line calling a dead-man's-
# switch ping service or a webhook curl to whatever chat/notification channel the operator uses.
[Unit]
Description=Alert for a failed %i

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/bin/logger -p daemon.crit -t runner-backup "backup run FAILED and exhausted its retries (%i) - check: journalctl -u runner-backup.service --since today"
